

Access Control List:
====================
sometimes you need to give specific permissions to a particular user or group without changing the file's owner or group. That's where ACL is useful.


-rw-r--r--+
          ↑
        ACL exists

Note:
====
Think of the mask as a filter:
1. If the mask allows more than the ACL entry, the ACL entry determines the effective permissions.
2. If the mask allows less than the ACL entry, the mask restricts the effective permissions.

Mathematically: Effective permissions= ACL entry "intersection"  Mask .  Here, intersection means the permissions both entries allow.


-m modifies or adds an ACL entry.
-x removes the specified ACL entry.

==========================================
Set 1: Add and remove access ACLs on files
==========================================
# Add access ACL entries
setfacl -m u:alice:rw report.txt        --> Alice's ACL entry will be added to the file
setfacl -m g:developers:rw report.txt   --> The developers group's ACL entry will be added to the file.

# View ACL
getfacl report.txt                      --> Displays the ACL entries for the file.


# Remove access ACL entries
setfacl -x u:alice report.txt       --> Alice's ACL entry will be removed from the file.
setfacl -x g:developers report.txt     --> The developers group's ACL entry will be removed from the file.


=================================================
set 2:  Apply and remove access ACLs recursively
=================================================

Uppercase X adds execute permission to directories and to files that already have at least one execute bit.

# Apply access ACLs recursively
setfacl -Rm u:alice:rwX folder      --> Alice's ACL entry will be added to the starting folder, its existing subdirectories, and existing files.
setfacl -Rm g:developers:rwX folder   --> The developers group's ACL entry will be added to the starting folder, its existing subdirectories, and existing files.


# View ACL
getfacl folder         --> Displays the ACL entries for the starting folder.


# Remove access ACL entries recursively
setfacl -Rx u:alice folder       --> Alice's ACL entry will be removed from the starting folder, its existing subdirectories, and existing files.
setfacl -Rx g:developers folder    --> The developers group's ACL entry will be removed from the starting folder, its existing subdirectories, and existing files.

===================================================
Set 3: Add and remove default ACLs on one directory
===================================================

d: = default/inheritance ACL
Uppercase X adds execute permission to directories and to files that already have at least one execute bit.

# Add default ACL entries
setfacl -m d:u:alice:rwX /var/www/project    --> Alice's default ACL entry will be added to the starting directory only, not directly to existing subdirectories and files.
                                             --> New subdirectories and files created inside the directory may inherit Alice's ACL permissions.
setfacl -m d:g:developers:rwX /var/www/project  --> The developers group's default ACL entry will be added to the starting directory only, not directly to existing subdirectories and files.
                                                --> New subdirectories and files created inside the directory may inherit the developers group's ACL permissions.


# View ACL
getfacl /var/www/project          --> Displays the access ACL and default ACL entries for the specified directory.


# Remove default ACL entries from this directory only
setfacl -x d:u:alice /var/www/project      --> Alice's default ACL entry will be removed from the starting directory, but not from existing subdirectories that already have that entry.
setfacl -x d:g:developers /var/www/project  --> The developers group's default ACL entry will be removed from the starting directory, but not from existing subdirectories that already have that entry.


================================================
set 4: Apply and remove default ACLs recursively
================================================

d: = default/inheritance ACL
Uppercase X adds execute permission to directories and to files that already have at least one execute bit.


# Apply default ACL entries recursively
setfacl -Rm d:u:alice:rwX /var/www/project     --> Alice's default ACL entry will be added to the starting directory and existing subdirectories, but not to regular files.
                                             --> New subdirectories and files created inside directories with this default ACL may inherit Alice's ACL permissions.
setfacl -Rm d:g:developers:rwX /var/www/project  --> The developers group's default ACL entry will be added to the starting directory and existing subdirectories, but not to regular files.
                                               --> New subdirectories and files created inside directories with this default ACL may inherit the developers group's ACL permissions.


# View ACL
getfacl /var/www/project                    --> Displays the access ACL and default ACL entries for the specified directory.

# Remove default ACL entries recursively
setfacl -Rx d:u:alice /var/www/project      -->  Alice's default ACL entry will be removed from the starting directory and existing subdirectories, but not from existing regular files.
setfacl -Rx d:g:developers /var/www/project  -->  The developers group's default ACL entry will be removed from the starting directory and existing subdirectories, but not from existing regular files.

================================================================================





# remove all extended ACL entries 
setfacl -b file          --> for file
setfacl -b directory     --> for directory, it won't remove for inside folders
setfacl -Rb directory    --> recursive, it will remove for inside folders also


# remove default ACL entries
setfacl -k directory    --> to remove default ACL for directory
setfacl -Rk directory    --> to remove default ACL for directory recursively, it will remove inside folders also


# remove both extended ACL entries & default ACL entries
setfacl -bk directory    --> to remove default ACL & extended ACL for directory
setfacl -Rbk directory    --> to remove default ACL & extended ACL for directory recursively


# to find all files & directories which have acl
find / -exec getfacl -s {} + 2>/dev/null | grep '^# file:'     -->  -s — skips files that have only the base ACL entries (no extended ACL entries).
